NNAAMMEE
       ibv_post_recv - post a list of work requests (WRs) to a receive queue

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ##iinncclluuddee <<iinnffiinniibbaanndd//vveerrbbss..hh>>

       iinntt iibbvv__ppoosstt__rreeccvv((ssttrruucctt iibbvv__qqpp _*_q_p,, ssttrruucctt iibbvv__rreeccvv__wwrr _*_w_r,,
                         ssttrruucctt iibbvv__rreeccvv__wwrr _*_*_b_a_d___w_r));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       iibbvv__ppoosstt__rreeccvv(())  posts  the linked list of work requests (WRs) starting
       with _w_r to the receive queue of the queue pair _q_p.  It stops processing
       WRs  from  this list at the first failure (that can be detected immedi‐
       ately while requests are being posted), and  returns  this  failing  WR
       through _b_a_d___w_r.

       The  argument  _w_r  is  an  ibv_recv_wr  struct,  as defined in <infini‐
       band/verbs.h>.

       struct ibv_recv_wr {
               uint64_t                wr_id;     /* User defined WR ID */
               struct ibv_recv_wr     *next;      /* Pointer to next WR in list, NULL if last WR */
               struct ibv_sge         *sg_list;   /* Pointer to the s/g array */
               int                     num_sge;   /* Size of the s/g array */
       };

       struct ibv_sge {
               uint64_t                addr;      /* Start address of the local memory buffer */
               uint32_t                length;    /* Length of the buffer */
               uint32_t                lkey;      /* Key of the local Memory Region */
       };

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       iibbvv__ppoosstt__rreeccvv(()) returns 0 on success, or the value of errno on  failure
       (which indicates the failure reason).

NNOOTTEESS
       The buffers used by a WR can only be safely reused after WR the request
       is fully executed and a work completion has  been  retrieved  from  the
       corresponding completion queue (CQ).

       If  the  QP  _q_p is associated with a shared receive queue, you must use
       the function iibbvv__ppoosstt__ssrrqq__rreeccvv(()), and not  iibbvv__ppoosstt__rreeccvv(()),  since  the
       QP's own receive queue will not be used.

       If  a WR is being posted to a UD QP, the Global Routing Header (GRH) of
       the incoming message will be placed in the first 40 bytes of  the  buf‐
       fer(s)  in the scatter list.  If no GRH is present in the incoming mes‐
       sage, then the first bytes will be undefined.  This means that  in  all
       cases,  the actual data of the incoming message will start at an offset
       of 40 bytes into the buffer(s) in the scatter list.

